    "result_text": "Tests are green. Here's how I addressed each finding:\n\n## Finding 1 \u2014 method name `service` \u2192 `audit`: **left as-is (finding is wrong for this repo)**\n\nThe AGENTS.md *worked example* uses verb-named methods, but the actual codebase does not: all 25 existing function classes declare `public void service(...)` \u2014 including the siblings in this exact pipeline (`SaveOwner`, `AssignOwnerCustomerCode`, `RespondWithOwnerCreated`). \"Reads as native to the codebase\" means matching the code, not the illustrative snippet. Renaming `AuditOwnerCreated.service` to `audit` would make it the single outlier out of 26 functions, so I kept `service`. (OfficeFloor takes the verb from the *class* name, which is already `AuditOwnerCreated` \u2014 the intent the reviewer wants is carried there.)\n\n## Finding 2 \u2014 `govern: [ transaction ]` on a non-writing step: **kept the tag, made the intent explicit**\n\nI agree the generic \"transaction = write\" prose doesn't obviously justify a tag on a logging step \u2014 but dropping it would break behaviour, and the repo's real convention already covers this case. `CheckOwnerTelephoneUnique` is a pure read (`findAll()`, no writes) yet is governed, as is every step from the first check onward. The POST pipeline is deliberately one continuous `transaction` governance so it commits once at the end; the `respond` step maps the persisted entity (including lazy relations) and relies on that boundary still being open. An ungoverned `audit` step wedged between `save` and `respond` would split the boundary and risk committing early / breaking lazy loading in `respond`.\n\nSo the tag isn't miscategorisation \u2014 it's the established single-commit-boundary pattern. I took the reviewer's alternative (\"make that intent explicit\") and added a Javadoc paragraph stating exactly why the step is governed despite touching no repository. That's the one concrete, behaviour-preserving improvement in the findings.\n\nNet change since the review: a documentation clarification only; acceptance behaviour and the AUDIT line (`id`, `customerCode`, `registrationDate`) are unchanged, and `AcceptanceTest` passes.",
